three ghosts and a time machine the  ending of better call saul didn't go  sci-fi on us but it does need some  explanation from that desperate escape  to the last cigarette here's everything  you need to know warning spoilers ahead  by better call saul's final episode  jimmy mcgill has lived under three names  and identities he starts as jimmy using  his birth name and is a hard-working  lawyer who seemingly can't resist a  shortcutter scheme he then becomes sol  goodman aiding and abetting the  murderous rise of drug kingpin walter  white finally we see him as gene takovic  the manager of a shopping mall cinnabon  who lives in constant fear of his  previous identities being discovered the  final episode sol gone gives each of  them the ending they deserve gene  takovic died as he lived hiding from the  police in a literal dumpster after  months of hiding in omaha buried under a  thick mustache and even thicker glasses  gene dips his toe back into the grifter  life after being recognized by  dim-witted taxi driver jeff however the  erstwhile slipping jimmy doesn't do  anything halfway his schemes become  riskier his true self comes through a  little too much and before long his  cover is completely blown by jeff's  youtube savvy mother marion played by  comedy legend carol burnett the final  four episodes in the series primarily  take place in the gene takavic era this  came as something of a surprise to  co-creator vince gilligan who told  rolling stone we were fascinated by  jimmy mcgill and then we wanted to see  more of gene takovic and omaha so we  kind of ran out the clock without even  meaning to and then we realized man the  first thing that could go was saul  goodman and that's the name of the show  it's only fitting that a man who was  once so good at elder law would finally  be brought to justice by an elder  much like gus spring gene is both a  career criminal and a steadfast and  reliable fast food employee even while  he's in the custody of the omaha police  gene uses his phone call to contact  cinnabon and let his staff know that he  won't be coming in ever again  this is how they get you  this is how they get you  facing in his holding cell like an  animal gene is furious with himself  after working as a cartel lawyer for  years he is finally caught because of a  small-time identity theft scheme lying  on the cold floor he glances up at a  crude carbon on the cell wall that says  my lawyer will ream your ass like a  switch being flipped gene takovic dies  and saul goodman is reborn gould who  wrote the script revealed that he views  jean saul and jimmy as three very  separate characters telling rolling  stone since this is an episode where he  goes from gene to saul and eventually  back to jimmy i was pretty careful to  use the name that felt right at every  moment i called those moments out in the  script  after months of hiding from the world  saul goodman returns the world's  greatest lawyer finds himself sitting at  a table across from a battalion of  hardened federal attorneys who are ready  to put him away for life plus 190 years  for working with walter white at his  side is bill oakley his favorite public  defender punching bag turned defense  attorney but only as an advisor saul  represents himself and his defense is a  doozy with pleading eyes and a trembling  voice he tells the collected prosecutors  that he worked for walter white under  fear of death lead prosecutor george  castellano asks if saul really thinks a  jury will believe his story saul  shrewdly responds  all i need is one  the scene reflects what many thought the  show would be from the beginning playing  on the character's comic relief and his  penchant for legal chicanery better call  saul did have plenty of that but few  expected jimmy mcgill to be such an  emotionally rich character least of all  the actor playing him in an interview  with the los angeles times bob odenkirk  described the start of his journey  playing the character saying so that  makes me as a beginning actor really  have to dig up a lot of stuff and feel a  lot of feelings from my own life in a  visceral way that left me feeling pretty  shagged out and ragged  the scene where saul lays out his victim  angle isn't all fun and games of course  saul is on the hook for money laundering  bribery and being an accessory after the  fact that dozens of murders including  those of hank trader and steve gomez on  hand to represent those victims is  hank's wife marie schrader played by  betsy brandt the pain of hank's death  clearly still weighs on marie and she  delivers a devastating monologue about  her husband saul's use of her real pain  to pivot into his own false narrative is  one of his most callous moments in the  entire series  they tell me they found you in a garbage  dumpster  well that makes sense  this was brandt's first appearance on  better call saul and her first time  playing marie since he ended breaking  bad nine years earlier she joined  several returning guest stars in the  finale including brian cranston as  walter and michael mckean is jimmy's  brother chuck when asked if there were  any other actors or characters from the  better call saul and breaking bad  universe he wanted to bring back for the  finale ghoul's answer was essentially  everyone as he told rolling stone i  would have loved to have patrick fabian  back to have dean norris back and a gun  would have been great if it fit in the  story i love our entire cast i'm greedy  we didn't want to make a kind of  overstuffed epic and i hope we didn't  just as saul returns to the law on his  own behalf so does kim first she  delivers her notarized confession to the  district attorney and howard's widow  cheryl hamlin in the penultimate episode  waterworks then she returns to her  purposely bland indecisive life in  central florida still the active  confession has changed something in kim  and she takes a tentative step forward  in her life by turning up at a legal aid  office kim's fate has been a driving  question for the series and its fans  from the very beginning kim was one of  the few main characters who did not  appear in breaking bad and many fans  feared the worst thinking kim might be  killed by the cartel however creator  peter gould told rolling stone that it  was important that kim's journey was her  own and not simply a detail in the jimmy  mcgill story  in the finale saul returns to an  albuquerque courtroom once again wearing  an outlandish suit viewers presume some  big twist is coming that saul has a  final stunt to pull and that he might  not end up serving the seven and a half  year sentence he fled down to there is  indeed a twist coming but not one that  anyone would have guessed saul begins  the hearing which is supposed to be a  formality to ratify his plea deal by  retelling the story of meeting walter  white for the first time fearing for his  life out in the desert then he stops and  for once tells the truth he kept working  for walt for 16 blood-soaked months  because he smelled money the scene also  invokes jimmy's emotional speech in  front of the bar association at the end  of season 4 which got his law license  reinstated and inflicted saul goodman  upon the world in both cases he is  playing to his audience using his real  emotions to affect the desired outcome  but this time it's an audience of one  and that's kim i just wanted her to come  here today i wanted her to hear this  interspersed with the main story of  three flashbacks to earlier times in  both better call saul and breaking bad  during these moments an important  question is considered  what would you do if you had a time  machine  a time machine  the first flashback takes us to the  season five episode bagman when jimmy  and mike face a harrowing two days in  the mexican desert hauling seven million  dollars in cash for lalo salamanca's  bail saul also poses the question to  walter white as the two of them await  being whisked away by ed the vacuum  repairman and the scene that takes place  during the breaking bad episode granite  state i'm lucky month from now best case  scenario i'm managing a cinnabon in  omaha gould revealed that he wanted the  flashbacks to show how jimmy is making a  change at long last telling rolling  stone the feeling i had about this  episode was that it was a little bit  like a christmas carol gene becomes saul  and he's visited by three ghosts and  each time he's visited by one of these  ghosts you realize this guy is trapped  in the cycle  while speaking to others jimmy's answers  to the time machine question are shallow  and facile but his real answer is found  in the episode's third flashback a  moment from the very beginning of better  call saul as jimmy delivers groceries to  his brother chuck chuck has taken a  leave of absence from the law firm he  founded with howard hamlin's father due  to a debilitating sensitivity to  electromagnetic waves in this moment of  brotherly kindness chuck makes a small  effort to reach out to jimmy but the  future saul goodman doesn't bite leaving  chuck to read hg wells as the time  machine by himself in the dark as much  as the relationship between jimmy and  kim has come to define the series for  the first three seasons his relationship  with chuck was arguably more important  in an interview with the los angeles  times odenkirk said of jimmy he's a guy  who is showing the audience his need his  hunger for love and respect from his  brother chuck his heart is open to chuck  and chuck crushes him it's impossible  not to wonder how different jimmy's life  would have been had his brother still  been alive  jimmy's truth-telling stunt gets him 86  years in prison but it turns out that  the prison isn't such a bad place for  saul goodman to end up  for one everyone calls him saul and he  gets fist bumps as he walks through the  halls he even gets to nurture his gene  takavik side by baking bread in the  prison cafeteria according to gould this  was always the natural end point for  jimmy as he told rolling stone this guy  has lived in the justice system he's  made a farce of it he's played it and it  just felt like that was the right place  to end the series him behind bars  jimmy may be behind bars but he's not  alone and kim even visits him posing as  his lawyer the two lean up against the  back wall of the room and share a  cigarette it's a quiet intimate scene  that evokes the scene the couple shared  in the pilot episode the cigarette burns  in color against the scene's film noir  inspired black and white filter  appropriately this was the final scene  that ray seahorn and bob odenkirk filmed  together and it's both the characters  and the actors saying goodbye after the  episode aired seahorn told vulture oh  what a great gift when your own emotions  match up with what your character is  supposed to be feeling  hi jimmy  the saga ends with a wistful look and a  pair of finger guns from behind bars as  kim leaves jimmy to serve his 86 year  sentence jimmy mcgill goes out on his  own terms atoning for his crimes and  closer to the woman he loves and he has  been in years still better call saul  leaves the audience with many questions  for instance will cheryl go through with  her plan to suit kim for her role in  howard's death i could sue you in civil  court i could take everything you've got  is jesse doing better on alaska than  gene did in omaha is this really the  last time jimmy and kim will see each  other will jimmy accept his prison fate  so easily or will the old slip and jimmy  come creeping up and are jimmy's enemies  dead or is there some associate of the  salamancas but is still out there with a  thirst for revenge we may never know the  answers to these questions but it was  still one hell of a finale  [Music]

